---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import { DatePickerCustomDemo, DatePickerDisabledDemo } from "../../../components/react-demos/date-picker";
import "@skryensya/core/components/date-picker.css";

const install = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/anchored.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/button.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/date-picker.css";`;

const nativeHtml = `<div class="sk-date-picker" lang="es-DO">
  <label class="sk-date-picker__label" for="arrival">Booking</label>
  <div class="sk-date-picker__control">
    <input
      class="sk-date-picker__input"
      id="arrival"
      lang="es-DO"
      name="arrival"
      type="date"
    />
  </div>
</div>`;

const customHtml = `<div
  class="sk-date-picker"
  data-sk-date-picker
  data-locale="es-DO"
  data-time-zone="America/Santo_Domingo"
  data-name="checkin"
>
  <label class="sk-date-picker__label" data-sk-date-picker-label>Booking</label>
  <div class="sk-date-picker__control sk-anchor" data-sk-date-picker-control>
    <input
      class="sk-date-picker__input"
      data-sk-date-picker-input
      placeholder="mm/dd/yyyy"
    />
    <button
      class="sk-date-picker__clear sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-sk-date-picker-clear
      data-icon-only
      data-size="sm"
      data-variant="ghost"
      type="button"
      aria-label="Clear date"
      hidden
    ><span data-sk-icon="close" data-sk-icon-size="sm"></span></button>
    <button
      class="sk-date-picker__trigger sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-sk-date-picker-trigger
      data-icon-only
      data-size="sm"
      data-variant="ghost"
      type="button"
      aria-label="Open calendar"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="calendar"></span>
    </button>
  </div>
</div>`;

const react = `import { DatePicker } from "@skryensya/react/date-picker";

function Example() {
  return (
    <DatePicker
      label="Booking"
      locale="es-DO"
      name="checkin"
      placeholder="mm/dd/yyyy"
      selectionMode="single"
      timeZone="America/Santo_Domingo"
    />
  );
}`;

const disabledHtml = `<div
  class="sk-date-picker"
  data-sk-date-picker
  data-disabled
  data-locale="en-US"
>
  <label class="sk-date-picker__label" data-sk-date-picker-label>Booking</label>
  <div class="sk-date-picker__control sk-anchor" data-sk-date-picker-control>
    <input
      class="sk-date-picker__input"
      data-sk-date-picker-input
      placeholder="mm/dd/yyyy"
    />
    <button
      class="sk-date-picker__trigger sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-sk-date-picker-trigger
      data-icon-only
      data-size="sm"
      data-variant="ghost"
      type="button"
      aria-label="Open calendar"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="calendar"></span>
    </button>
  </div>
</div>`;

const disabledReact = `import { DatePicker } from "@skryensya/react/date-picker";

function Example() {
  return <DatePicker label="Booking" locale="en-US" placeholder="mm/dd/yyyy" disabled />;
}`;
---

<Base
  title="DatePicker"
  description="Single-date or date-range field, with native type=date and a custom calendar behind a trigger."
>
  <h1>DatePicker</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Two versions, one field. The native <strong>native</strong> version is an <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code>
    with system chrome: OS keyboard, form and calendar come from the platform, without
    JavaScript. The <strong>custom</strong> mounts a <a href="/en/components/calendar">Calendar</a> behind
    of a trigger with the same field shape, for when you need range, explicit location or a
    Consistent calendar across browsers. The two share <code>.sk-date-picker__control</code>,
    so they look the same. The calendar itself — header, views, grids — is the responsibility of
    Calendar, not this component: DatePicker just nests it inside its popover.
  </p>

  <h2>Native</h2>
  <p>Platform control, without JavaScript or enhancer.</p>
  <ComponentPreview html={nativeHtml} label="Native DatePicker" />

  <h2>Custom</h2>
  <p>The custom field and its popup calendar live in a separate demo.</p>
  {/* `overlay` (~24rem): the calendar reserves the height of its six rows inside the frame. */}
  <ComponentPreview html={customHtml} react={react} label="DatePicker custom" viewport="overlay">
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <DatePickerCustomDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2>Disabled</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data-disabled</code> on the root configures the machine and Zag distributes the state on its
    own: <code>data-disabled</code> on <code>sk-date-picker__control</code>, and a native
    <code>disabled</code> on the input and on the trigger. But each of those three read its
    color/background/border from a hook
    (<code>--sk-date-picker-fg</code>/<code>-bg</code>/<code>-border-color</code>) declared
    unconditionally, so disabling the field changed nothing about what those hooks resolved to — the
    field still read as interactive. The rule now lives in a single place,
    <code>.sk-date-picker__control[data-disabled]</code>, which rewrites those three hooks: the input
    and the trigger inherit them with no rules of their own.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview html={disabledHtml} react={disabledReact} label="DatePicker (disabled)">
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <DatePickerDisabledDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentPreview>

  <h2>Which one to use</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the <strong>native</strong>: it is the one that works without JS and the one that the operating system already
    knows how to present on each platform. I upgraded to <strong>custom</strong> only when the native one is not enough:
    selection of <em>range</em>, a time zone and a locale that cannot be left as an ambiguous string, or
    a calendar that looks the same in all browsers. It is the same decision as Select native vs.
    Select custom.
  </p>

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <p>
    React delivers <code>DatePicker</code> on the same machine as <code>Calendar</code> (<code>@zag-js/date-picker</code>), and reuses the same calendar body: there are no two grid implementations. Vanilla hydrates the authored <code>data-sk-date-picker</code>: Patches the control and renders the calendar with the Calendar shared component. The native <code>type="date"</code> input does not need an enhancer.
  </p>

  <h2>On mobile</h2>
  <p>
    Under the desktop breakpoint (30rem) the custom calendar stops hanging from the field and becomes
    a <strong>bottom sheet</strong>: the positioner fills the screen as scrim and the content is
    anchor to the bottom edge, full width, and go up. Take the <em>skin</em> of the Vaul, not the drag
    —it is a Zag popover, not a native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>—, but it closes the same when playing outside or
    with <kbd class="sk-kbd">Esc</kbd>. It is the same responsive slice as <a href="/en/components/dialog">Dialog Vaul</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    Date uses <code>DateValue</code> values and explicit time zone; It is not modeled as an ambiguous locale string. The control retains its accessible name <code>label</code>. The accessibility of the grid itself (role, keyboard navigation, view switching) is a contract of <a href="/en/components/calendar">Calendar</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>CSS</h2>
  <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
  <StylingHooks
    groups={[
      { file: "components/date-picker.css", probe: "sk-date-picker", title: "DatePicker" },
      { file: "components/calendar.css", probe: "sk-calendar", title: "Inherited from Calendar" },
      { file: "patterns/anchored.css", probe: "sk-anchored", title: "Anchor Inherits" },
      { file: "components/button.css", probe: "sk-button", title: "Inherited from Button" },
    ]}
  />
</Base>
